  "slug": "equilibrium-properties-mean-f31f",
  "summary": "Eight preprints pair classical simulation limits with task-specific quantum algorithms for sensing, search, sampling and Ising optimization.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is algorithm-level analysis of where quantum or analog hardware offers advantage and where classical methods suffice. On the classical side, F2-linear partitioning scales Pauli propagation beyond 10^11 Pauli strings, and window propagation gives a provably polynomial-time approximation of first-order OTOCs in 1D Haar-random brickwork circuits. On the quantum side, task-specific sensing algorithms achieve a sqrt(N) speedup for discrete-frequency spectroscopy, and a unified framework for AC field sensing reports pulse protocols that beat dynamical decoupling. Hardware-facing work includes a gate-based realization of deterministic quantum-walk search, a photonic scheme for fluid-flow modeling, and an opto-electronic Ising machine with 100,000 spins read out in a single shot. A complexity analysis of bosonic Lindbladian fixed points addresses stationary-state estimation and sampling.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-24"
}
